{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`java.security.KeyPairGenerator` is the Java Cryptography Architecture engine class for generating public/private key pairs for a selected algorithm. The documented flow is to obtain an instance with `getInstance`, optionally initialize it, then call `generateKeyPair()` (or `genKeyPair()`).","The reported value `Ed25519` refers to the algorithm argument bound by the CrySL rule's `getInstance(algorithm)` or `getInstance(algorithm, _)` event. It does not, from the rule text, refer to a generated `KeyPair`, an encoded key format, or an integer key-size argument.","The cited CrySL source evaluates the allowlist condition `algorithm in {"RSA", "EC", "DSA", "DiffieHellman", "DH"};`. Therefore, `Ed25519` does not satisfy that particular rule-file allowlist.","This CrySL allowlist is not a Java API validity rule. Oracle's Java Security Standard Algorithm Names specification lists `Ed25519` as a `KeyPairGenerator` algorithm for Ed25519 Edwards-curve signature key pairs.","The `KeyPairGenerator` API documents `getInstance(String)` and provider-selecting overloads. Without explicitly selecting a provider, the implementation searches registered providers; lack of supporting providers results in `NoSuchAlgorithmException`.","The API documents initialization by integer key size, optionally with a `SecureRandom`, or by algorithm-specific `AlgorithmParameterSpec`, optionally with a `SecureRandom`. Key size is algorithm-specific, and an unsupported size can cause `InvalidParameterException`.","The API permits generation without explicit initialization, in which case the provider supplies documented defaults. It warns that defaults can differ between providers and can change across releases, and recommends explicit initialization rather than relying on provider defaults.","The Java API does not document that `Ed25519` is unsafe, does not adopt the CrySL allowlist, and does not recommend replacing `Ed25519` with RSA, EC, DSA, DiffieHellman, or DH. Its directly relevant documented recommendation is explicit initialization when provider-default behavior would otherwise be relied upon.","For compatibility, the Java Security Standard Algorithm Names specification documents `X.509` as an ASN.1 `SubjectPublicKeyInfo` public-key format and `PKCS#8` as an ASN.1 `PrivateKeyInfo` private-key format. These are format-name definitions; `KeyPairGenerator` itself does not define an application network protocol or an algorithm-migration procedure.","RFC 8410 defines Ed25519's algorithm identifier/OID (`1.3.101.112`) for relevant ASN.1 public-key, private-key, certificate, and signature structures. Thus, in those structures, algorithm identity is represented as part of the encoded structure.","Neither the `KeyPairGenerator` API documentation nor the cited Java standard-names material documents a generic conversion of an existing key pair from another algorithm to Ed25519, preservation of key bytes/certificates/signatures after an algorithm change, or a general wire-protocol migration path. Compatibility remains dependent on the particular key consumer, format/profile, peer capabilities, provider, and cryptographic operation.","Current Java SE standard-name documentation recognizes `Ed25519` as a `KeyPairGenerator` algorithm. Oracle JDK 15 documentation also identifies EdEC key-spec APIs as introduced in 15 and documents SunEC support for Ed25519 key-pair generation. Availability is nevertheless provider- and runtime-dependent; recognition of a standard algorithm name is not by itself a guarantee that every selected provider supports it.","The exact CrySL matching declaration in the cited source is:
`SPEC java.security.KeyPairGenerator

OBJECTS
	java.lang.String algorithm;
	java.security.KeyPair keyPair;
	java.security.spec.AlgorithmParameterSpec params;
	int keysize;

EVENTS
	g1: getInstance(algorithm);
	g2: getInstance(algorithm, _);
	Get := g1 | g2;

	i1: initialize(keysize);
	i2: initialize(keysize, _);
	i3: initialize(params);
	i4: initialize(params, _);
	Init := i1 | i2 | i3 | i4;

	k1: keyPair = generateKeyPair();
	k2: keyPair = genKeyPair();
	Gen := k1 | k2;`","The quoted CrySL declaration scopes the specification to `java.security.KeyPairGenerator`. It lists `getInstance`, `initialize`, `generateKeyPair`, and `genKeyPair`; it constrains the listed calls by their arity and bound argument/result variables. In particular, it lists one- and two-argument forms for `getInstance` and `initialize`, with `_` denoting the unbound second argument, and zero-argument generation methods whose result is bound to `keyPair`.","The source's relevant reported condition is quoted verbatim as `algorithm in {"RSA", "EC", "DSA", "DiffieHellman", "DH"};`. The source additionally declares algorithm-dependent key-size conditions and required `preparedRSA`, `preparedDSA`, `preparedDH`, and `preparedEC` predicates for parameter-spec paths.","A method merely sharing one of those names is not within the declared specification scope solely by name: the source declares `SPEC java.security.KeyPairGenerator`, rather than a global, name-only specification. The CrySL file itself does not provide further implementation-level matcher semantics beyond that type declaration and its event patterns."],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official Java API documentation for the governed class: factory selection, provider behavior, initialization, generation, exceptions, defaults, and the recommendation to initialize explicitly rather than rely on provider defaults.","limitations":"It does not prescribe a migration between key algorithms, define an application wire format, or label Ed25519 unsafe.","claim":"`KeyPairGenerator` generates key pairs; it supports `getInstance`, initialization, and `generateKeyPair`/`genKeyPair`, and recommends explicit initialization because provider defaults may vary or change.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/security/KeyPairGenerator.html"},{"relevance":"Official Java Security Standard Algorithm Names specification for the recognition of `Ed25519` as a `KeyPairGenerator` algorithm and for standard key-format names.","limitations":"A recognized standard name is not a universal provider-availability guarantee and this specification does not supply a generic migration procedure.","claim":"`Ed25519` is a documented `KeyPairGenerator` algorithm; `X.509` and `PKCS#8` identify standard public- and private-key encoding formats.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html"},{"relevance":"Primary source for the exact declared CrySL specification, events, type scope, and rule-level algorithm condition.","limitations":"The URL is on the repository's moving `master` branch. It does not identify the precise commit or CryptoAnalysis implementation/version that produced the reported result, and the file does not itself explain all matcher implementation details.","claim":"The specification is declared as `SPEC java.security.KeyPairGenerator`; its events include `getInstance`, `initialize`, `generateKeyPair`, and `genKeyPair`; and it constrains `algorithm` to `RSA`, `EC`, `DSA`, `DiffieHellman`, or `DH`.","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/KeyPairGenerator.crysl"},{"relevance":"Primary standards source for Ed25519 algorithm identifiers in ASN.1 key, certificate, and signature structures.","limitations":"It covers specified ASN.1/PKIX-related structures, not every possible application protocol or a general key-algorithm migration process.","claim":"Ed25519 has OID `1.3.101.112` and is defined for identified public-key, private-key, certificate, and signature structures.","authority":"primary","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8410.html"},{"relevance":"Official Oracle JDK 15 API documentation relevant to the Java-version introduction of EdEC key specification APIs.","limitations":"This is Oracle JDK documentation for a particular release; it does not establish that every provider on every runtime supports Ed25519.","claim":"`EdECPrivateKeySpec` is documented as available since Java 15.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/15/docs/api/java.base/java/security/spec/EdECPrivateKeySpec.html"},{"relevance":"Official Oracle JDK 15 security documentation referenced for provider-specific Ed25519 support.","limitations":"Provider support and initialization restrictions described there are provider- and release-specific rather than cross-provider JCA guarantees.","claim":"Oracle's JDK 15 SunEC provider documentation includes Ed25519/Ed448/EdDSA KeyPairGenerator support.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/15/security/security-developer-guide.pdf"}],"queries_performed":["Reviewed the official Java SE 25 `java.security.KeyPairGenerator` API documentation for governed functionality, initialization semantics, provider/default behavior, exceptions, and recommendations.","Reviewed the official Java SE 25 Java Security Standard Algorithm Names specification for `Ed25519` KeyPairGenerator recognition and `X.509`/`PKCS#8` format-name definitions.","Reviewed the supplied primary CrySL source URL for the exact `SPEC` declaration, event clauses, argument patterns, and algorithm constraint.","Reviewed RFC 8410 for Ed25519 identifiers and ASN.1 encoding contexts relevant to persisted key and certificate structures.","Reviewed Oracle JDK 15 documentation for version/provider-specific EdEC and SunEC support context."],"externally_unresolved_questions":["Which exact Crypto-API-Rules commit and which CryptoAnalysis version generated the reported result? The cited rule URL tracks `master`, so equivalence to the executed detector rule cannot be confirmed from the available material.","Which Java runtime version, provider, and `KeyPairGenerator` overload are used in the affected usage? Those facts are necessary to determine actual provider availability and applicable initialization behavior.","Whether initialization is omitted, uses an integer key size, or uses an `AlgorithmParameterSpec`, and what exact values or parameter provenance are present. The rule contains separate size and prepared-parameter predicates, but the reported allowlist message only establishes the algorithm-value mismatch.","What consumes the generated key pair, whether keys/certificates/signatures are persisted, and which protocol peers or format profiles must interoperate. The cited API and standards sources do not provide a generic compatibility or migration path without those facts."]}
